\item When \code{tau} is \code{NULL}, standard/symmetric forms are used (for
\code{"expectile"} and \code{"smooth_pinball"}, \code{tau = 0.5} is used internally).
\item When \code{tau} is provided (scalar or numeric vector in \verb{[0, 1]}),
asymmetric/quantile versions are applied across the specified quantile
levels.
\item Note that \code{loss_name = "mse"} with a non-\code{NULL} \code{tau} computes the
asymmetric expectile loss (\code{expectile_loss}), not a quantile (pinball)
loss; use \code{loss_name = "mae"} (or \code{"smooth_pinball"}) with \code{tau} for
quantile regression.
\item If a validation set (\code{x_val} and \code{y_val}) is provided, validation
loss is evaluated every \code{epoch_step} epochs and passed to \code{callback}.
At the end of training, if \code{callback} stores a best state, those weights
are restored into the returned model.
\item Number of \pkg{torch} threads is temporarily set to \code{1} during
training and restored on exit.
}
